{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are systematically too high versus realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward; apply a downward level shift that increases with horizon.",
    "Trend/mean-reversion miscalibration: in downtrends the model assumes rebound/sideways too early; enforce stronger mean reversion downward (i.e., keep declines going longer) when the last 2–3 weeks show a clear falling slope.",
    "Overshoot after short-term upticks: after brief rises in the forecast path (local spikes), realized prices do not follow and continue lower; damp forecast peaks and reduce post-spike levels (cap daily upward moves).",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: near-term (days 1–7) errors are small, but medium/long horizon (days ~15–30) errors widen materially; use a ramping correction (e.g., subtract more €/t each week).",
    "Volatility overestimation in the tail: forecast paths show higher variance and higher terminal levels than realized; shrink deviations toward a lower trendline and reduce tail volatility."
  ]
}
